## How Do You Choose the Right Therapist?

The therapist you work with matters at least as much as the room where therapy takes place.

When considering a therapist, ask yourself:

### Do I feel respected?

You should be able to discuss your concerns without feeling judged or dismissed.

### Can I communicate honestly?

You may eventually discuss personal or uncomfortable subjects. Consider whether you feel able to be reasonably open with this therapist.

### Do we have shared goals?

Therapy should involve a collaborative understanding of what you are working toward. The APA emphasizes collaboration and agreement around treatment goals as important aspects of the therapeutic relationship.

### Does the therapist understand my concerns?

Different therapists have different areas of experience, approaches, and populations they work with. Ask about their experience with the concerns you want to address.

### Does the practical arrangement work?

Consider location, scheduling, cost, insurance, transportation, and whether you can realistically attend appointments consistently.

## Common Misconceptions About In-Person Therapy

### "In-person therapy is always better."

Not necessarily. Evidence comparing delivery formats does not support a simple conclusion that face-to-face therapy is universally more effective.

### "I need to know exactly what to talk about before therapy."

You don't. Your therapist can help organize the conversation and identify areas worth exploring.

### "My first therapist has to be the right therapist."

Not always. Finding a good therapeutic fit can take time. The APA notes that the therapeutic relationship is important and that it is reasonable to discuss concerns about the fit or consider another therapist when necessary.

### "Going to therapy means something is wrong with me."

Seeking counseling can simply mean that you want professional support while dealing with something difficult or while trying to make changes in your life.

People seek therapy for many different reasons, including relationship challenges, life transitions, stress, grief, anxiety, depression, and personal growth.

## When Might Professional Mental Health Support Be Useful?

Consider speaking with a qualified mental health professional if emotional or behavioral concerns are interfering with your relationships, work, school, daily responsibilities, or overall quality of life.

You do not have to wait until a problem becomes overwhelming before asking for help.

A therapist can help you explore what you are experiencing, clarify goals, and determine whether counseling is an appropriate form of support.

This article provides general educational information and is not a diagnosis or a substitute for individualized professional mental health care. If you are experiencing an immediate mental health emergency or believe you may be in danger, seek emergency or crisis support in your area rather than relying on an online article.

